meibo (名簿) is an identity lifecycle and single sign-on server built for
the way Japanese companies and schools actually work.

It keeps one authoritative roster of people and their affiliations, and drives
everything else from it:

  * Lifecycle as a state machine.  Joining, enrolling, taking leave of absence,
    returning, resigning, withdrawing and graduating are modelled as explicit
    transitions.  Corporate and academic organisations share the machine but get
    different vocabulary (休職 vs 休学, 退職 vs 退学).  Effective dates are
    honoured: a 4/1 hire is provisioned the night before, a 3/31 resignation is
    reclaimed the morning after.

  * Japanese name handling.  Family/given names are kept separate, with kana and
    Hepburn romaji alongside, plus former surname (旧姓) and preferred name, so
    the same person renders correctly in every downstream SaaS.

  * Identity provider.  OpenID Connect (authorization code + PKCE only) and
    SAML 2.0, with presets for Google Workspace, Microsoft 365 federation and
    GakuNin (学認).

  * Multi-factor authentication.  Passkeys (WebAuthn) and TOTP, with recovery
    codes.

  * Provisioning.  Inbound SCIM 2.0 and CSV import from HR or student
    information systems; outbound to Google Workspace, Microsoft Graph and any
    SCIM 2.0 endpoint.

  * STNS v2 API, so the same roster can serve POSIX accounts and SSH keys to
    NetBSD and Linux hosts without running LDAP.

  * torii, an identity-aware proxy, so internal web applications can be exposed
    without an intranet perimeter.

meibo is a set of static Go binaries with PostgreSQL as its only runtime
dependency.
